  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 135 PM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nThis Afternoon - Thursday: Storm Potential with Seasonable \nTemperatures\n\nStorms from this morning shot off a boundary that went northeast \nthrough the CWA. This boundary helped to initiate some showers across \nwestern and central Wisconsin and continued to move northward. More \nshowers and storms are possible this afternoon and evening along \nthis boundary, however the chances are low (<15%). As we head into \ntonight and Wednesday morning, another MCS or line of storms is \nexpected to form as a shortwave shifts down from the northern Plains \ninto portions of the Upper Midwest. Due to this morning's MCS, the \npath for tonight's storms should stay mostly south of the forecast \narea with our southern tier of counties seeing the highest potential \nfor showers/storms (20 to 40%). The RRFS solution has one of the \nmore defined storm complexes and it impacts northeast Iowa. It is \nalso one of the more northern solutions. Not to rule out the \npossibility of this happening, but this morning's MCS went a little \nfurther south than anticipated so the thinking is that Wednesday \nmorning's would follow suit. In addition, a boundary should form to \nthe north of the storm complex after it moves through, leaving a low \npotential (15 to 25%) for scattered showers and storms mostly north \nof I-90 during the afternoon and evening.\n\nBy early Thursday morning another shortwave moves down into the \nregion. At the same time, a low forms in the central Plains. This \nlow will shift towards the northeast as southwest flow increases \naloft and the shortwave will move southeastward. This two systems \nlook to meet somewhere between central Iowa and southern Minnesota. \nThis area would have the highest chance for showers and storms to \noccur. Currently there is still a bit of uncertainty on where \nexactly showers and storms pass through for Thursday morning. Will \nget better details as we get closer. With zonal flow anticipated \nthrough Thursday, high temperatures should stay close to normal, \nwith highs in the mid 70s to mid 80s.\n\nFriday - Early Next Week: Periodic Storm Chances\n\nHeading into the weekend guidance is in good agreement on the ridge \nshifting over the eastern US. This in turn will put our region in \nwest to southwest flow as the ridge axis should stay to our \nsoutheast. The position of this ridge will help keep our \ntemperatures slightly below normal with highs in the low 70s to low \n80s through the weekend. With a good southwest fetch of moisture \nfrom the Gulf from Friday into Sunday and PWATs in the 97.5 to 99th \npercentiles (1.75 to 2.25\") there is a heavy rain potential during \nthe period. Both the GFS and ECMWF have a warm front that brings an \ninitial band of showers and storms on Friday night/Saturday morning \nfollowed by another shortwave for Saturday evening. There is already \nan EFI signal of 0.5 to 0.65 indicating that this rain event would \nlean on the higher side of climatology. WPC has gone ahead and \nissued a slight risk for excessive rainfall for Saturday due to \nthese parameters.",
